OBSERVATIONS (non-blocking, for the lead's backlog — none block RF1):
- Active desktop nav-tab (`bg-white/20` over the accent-strong gradient) is 4.19:1 for
  its white label text, just under the 4.5:1 normal-text floor — identical on master,
  unowned by any RF1 file (only spacing/whitespace classes changed on that element).
  Pre-existing WCAG 1.4.3 near-miss, not introduced by this run.
- Dashboard "Target $11351" chart-annotation text is a low ~3.1:1 in dark mode on both
  branch and master (dashboard.js-authored annotation, not an RF1 file); RF1's stone
  remap left it marginally better (3.04 -> 3.14), not worse.
- What-If "$" input-prefix span (`text-gray-600`, no `dark:` twin) stays low-contrast
  in dark mode on both branch and master (~1.9-2.0:1); RF1 didn't introduce or fix this
  (whatif.html not in RF1's manifest).
- Reflow tolerance basis for the deferred /whatif 390px overflow (item 6 above) is
  ambiguous in RF-RUN-SPEC's acceptance wording between "6.25%+8px of the reported
  scrollWidth" (my reading, PASS) vs "...of the overflow-amount delta" (would FAIL);
  worth a one-line clarification in a future spec so this isn't re-litigated per
  attempt.
- axe's default color-contrast rule does not evaluate Plotly's SVG `<text>` fill at
  all (confirmed: it flags nothing on any chart-bearing page in either theme, on
  either branch or master, while the manual contrast walk does inspect those nodes
  once the `fill`-vs-`color` property distinction is handled) — this is why point 2's
  manual walk, not axe, is load-bearing for chart-label contrast; noting it because a
  future checker relying on axe alone for chart pages would silently miss this class
  of defect.
